    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-uttara-kanda-0024",
      "sequence": 1007,
      "meaning": "The Lord performs millions of Ashvamedha sacrifices and gives countless gifts to Brahmins. He protects the path of scripture, bears the weight of dharma, remains beyond the qualities and yet enjoys splendour like Indra. Sita is always devoted to her husband—a courteous, humble treasury of beauty who knows the ocean of compassion's sovereignty and serves his lotus feet with concentrated mind. Although the household has many skilled male and female attendants, she personally performs its service and follows Ramachandra's direction. Knowing the manner that pleases him, Shri herself serves accordingly. Without pride in status, she serves Kausalya and all the other mothers-in-law in their homes. Shiva tells Uma that Sita is Rama (the goddess Lakshmi), worshipped by Brahma and others, Mother of the world and forever beyond reproach.",
